  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday night through Thursday)\nIssued at 211 AM EDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\nThrough Sunday, prevailing flow will be out of the north as a \ndeveloping nor'easter in the New England region stalls before \neventually getting pushes out. This setup will keep drier air in the \natmospheric column along with inhibition of deep convection through \nSunday. While some positive vorticity impulses will stream across \nthe area and the diurnal sea breezes will still form, these alone \nmay not be enough to overcome the inhibition created by the dry air. \nNevertheless, some air parcels will be capable of lifting high \nenough to produce some isolated to scattered showers and possible a \ncouple embedded storms but they will be of a weaker variety.\n\nBy early next week, the system in the northeast US will shift more \nnortwards as a mid-to-upper level shortwave trough pushes across the \ncentral US and deepens across the Mid-Atlantic and Southeast US \nregions. As this trough swings through the Florida Peninsula, low \nlevel flow will shift back to a southerly to southwesterly direction \nand result in the return of tropical moisture advection. With deeper \nmoisture arriving and this trough providing some upper level forcing \nfor ascent, rain chances will increase back to the 60-80% range by \nTuesday and into the middle of next week. Any potential impacts will \nbe assessed later on since this is at the end of the forecast period \nand uncertainty is high. \n\nHigh temperatures each day are still expected in the upper 80s to \naround 90 each day for most areas. Overnight lows will be a few \ndegrees cooler through Monday night with the front clearing the \narea, with lows near the lake region in the low to mid 60s and the \nrest of the region seeing lows in the upper 60s to low 70s through \nMonday night. By Tuesday night, warmer overnight lows will return.",
